se environments, boasting superior theoretical speeds compared to Padrão 802.11ac (Wi-Fi 5) [Data: Relationships (1394)]. While 802.11ac offered significant speed increases, 802.11ax prioritizes the ability to handle more traffic concurrently and with lower latency, making it ideal for the growing Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystem and applications requiring consistent, high-bandwidth connectivity.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Technological Features Driving Performance\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The evolution of Wi-Fi standards within this community is driven by the introduction and refinement of key technologies. MIMO (Multiple-Input Multiple-Output) was a significant advancement introduced with Padrão 802.11n (Wi-Fi 4), enabling multiple antennas to transmit and receive data simultaneously, thereby increasing throughput and range [Data: Entities (988)]. This technology was crucial in providing a noticeable performance boost over earlier standards.\\n\\nSubsequent standards built upon and enhanced these capabilities. Padrão 802.11ac (Wi-Fi 5) leveraged MU-MIMO (Multi-User MIMO), which allows an access point to communicate with multiple devices simultaneously, significantly improving network efficiency in environments with many connected users [Data: Entities (989)]. Padrão 802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6/6E) further refines MU-MIMO and introduces OFDMA (Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiple Access), which divides a channel into smaller sub-channels to serve multiple users concurrently with different data rates, optimizing efficiency and reducing latency, especially in dense network conditions [Data: Entities (990)]. These technological innovations are directly responsible for the exponential growth in Wi-Fi performance and capability observed across the standards.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the fundamental role these Wi-Fi standards play in modern communicati